"""
NCERT Class 5 Mathematics - Chapter 4: We the Travellers - II Data
"""

slides_data = [{'id': 1, 'heading': 'Equal Sums', 'tagline': 'Balancing Number Groups with Minimal Swaps', 'color': 'from-amber-500 to-orange-600', 'badge': 'Class 5 Maths • Chapter 4', 'audio_text': 'Welcome, young travellers and mathematicians! Today we begin our journey into We the Travellers - II. We start with a fascinating balancing puzzle from our textbook: making sums equal. Imagine you have two groups of number cards. Group A has numbers whose sum is larger than Group B. Our goal is to make the sum of both groups exactly equal by swapping pairs of numbers with the fewest moves possible! To solve this scientifically, first calculate the sum of each group and find their difference. If the difference between Group A and Group B is D, then swapping a card from Group A with a card from Group B must have a difference of exactly D divided by 2! For example, if Group A sums to 48 and Group B sums to 40, the difference is 8. We must find a card in Group A that is 8 divided by 2, or 4 units larger than a card in Group B. When we swap these two cards, both groups balance perfectly at 44! This algebraic secret helps us balance loads on transport vehicles, cargo ships, and airplane cargo holds every single day.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Balancing Rule', 'content': 'To balance two groups with difference D in a single swap, find a number in the larger group that exceeds a number in the smaller group by exactly D / 2.', 'icon': '⚖️'}, {'title': 'Target Equilibrium Sum', 'content': 'The balanced target sum is always the total combined sum of all cards divided by 2. Total Sum = Sum(A) + Sum(B); Target = Total / 2.', 'icon': '🎯'}, {'title': 'Real-World Balance', 'content': 'Aircraft luggage loaders and ship captains use this exact logic to distribute cargo evenly between left and right holds to ensure safe travel.', 'icon': '✈️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Group A sums to 50 and Group B sums to 40. What must be the difference between the two swapped numbers to balance both groups?', 'options': ['5 (Difference of 10 divided by 2)', '10', '20', '2'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Total difference is 50 - 40 = 10. The swapped numbers must differ by 10 / 2 = 5 to make both groups equal to 45.'}, {'question': "If Group A has 25 and Group B has 17, and all other cards are identical, how much larger is Group A's sum?", 'options': ['8', '4', '16', '12'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 25 - 17 = 8. Swapping them changes the group balance by 8.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'heading': 'Fuel Mileage', 'tagline': 'Comparing Fuel Needs and Eco-Friendly Travel', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Fuel & Conservation Arithmetic', 'audio_text': 'Every vehicle that carries travellers requires energy, usually in the form of petrol, diesel, or electricity. Different vehicles consume vastly different amounts of fuel depending on their engine power, weight, and the number of passengers they carry. A lightweight motorcycle can travel up to 50 kilometres on a single litre of petrol! A family car might travel 15 kilometres per litre, while a school bus or large truck might travel only 4 to 6 kilometres per litre of diesel. However, notice something crucial: although a bus consumes more fuel than a motorcycle, it carries 50 students at once! When we divide the fuel consumed by the number of travellers, public transport like buses and electric trains is far more efficient per passenger than single-occupant cars. Conserving fuel saves money, reduces harmful exhaust emissions, and keeps our air clean for everyone. Let us calculate vehicle fuel consumption and explore eco-wise choices.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Fuel Consumption Comparison', 'content': 'Motorbike: ~50 km/L (1-2 persons); Car: ~15 km/L (4-5 persons); Minibus: ~8 km/L (15-20 persons); Diesel Bus: ~4 km/L (50 persons).', 'icon': '⛽'}, {'title': 'Per-Passenger Efficiency', 'content': 'A bus using 10 litres to travel 40 km carries 50 people. That is 0.2 litres per student! Cars carry far fewer passengers for the fuel burned.', 'icon': '🚌'}, {'title': 'Eco-Friendly Driving', 'content': 'Smooth driving, proper tire pressure, turning off engines at traffic signals, and using public transit minimize pollution and conserve petroleum.', 'icon': '🌱'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If a car travels 15 kilometres on 1 litre of petrol, how many kilometres can it travel on 6 litres?', 'options': ['90 km', '60 km', '75 km', '120 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Distance = Fuel × Mileage = 6 litres × 15 km/L = 90 km.'}, {'question': 'Why is travelling by public bus more fuel-efficient per person than driving multiple cars?', 'options': ['One bus carries up to 50 passengers, reducing per-person fuel used', 'Buses do not consume any fuel at all', 'Buses travel at the speed of light', 'Buses only travel downhill'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Spreading the fuel consumption over 50 passengers dramatically cuts fuel use per traveller.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'heading': 'Place Value', 'tagline': 'Adding 2-Digit and 3-Digit Numbers with Column Regrouping', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Addition with Regrouping', 'audio_text': 'When calculating travel distances and fuel bills, we frequently add 2-digit and 3-digit numbers using place value tables! In our base-ten number system, every column has a strict capacity of nine units. The moment we gather 10 Ones, they bundle together into 1 Ten, which carries over into the Tens column! Similarly, when 10 Tens accumulate, they bundle into 1 Hundred and carry over into the Hundreds column. Let us add 258 and 175. First, align the digits strictly in their columns: Hundreds, Tens, and Ones. Add the Ones: 8 plus 5 equals 13 Ones. We write 3 in the Ones column and carry over 1 Ten to the Tens column. Now add the Tens: 5 plus 7 equals 12, plus the carried 1 Ten gives 13 Tens! We write 3 in the Tens column and carry over 1 Hundred. Finally, add the Hundreds: 2 plus 1 equals 3, plus the carried 1 gives 4 Hundreds. The total sum is 433! Column alignment and systematic carrying make large additions error-free.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Regrouping Law', 'content': '10 Ones bundle into 1 Ten (carries left); 10 Tens bundle into 1 Hundred; 10 Hundreds bundle into 1 Thousand.', 'icon': '📦'}, {'title': 'Strict Column Alignment', 'content': 'Always align digits from right to left starting at the Ones place: Ones under Ones, Tens under Tens, Hundreds under Hundreds.', 'icon': '📊'}, {'title': 'Step-by-Step Addition', 'content': 'Step 1: Add Ones, carry over any 10s. Step 2: Add Tens including carried ten. Step 3: Add Hundreds including carried hundred.', 'icon': '✍️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the sum of 348 and 275?', 'options': ['623', '613', '523', '633'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Ones: 8+5=13 (write 3, carry 1); Tens: 4+7+1=12 (write 2, carry 1); Hundreds: 3+2+1=6. Result = 623.'}, {'question': 'When adding the Ones column, you get 16. What digit is written in the Ones place, and what is carried over?', 'options': ['Write 6, carry over 1 Ten', 'Write 1, carry over 6 Tens', 'Write 16 in the Ones place', 'Carry over 16 to Hundreds'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 16 Ones = 1 Ten and 6 Ones. Write 6, carry 1.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'heading': 'Inverse Operations', 'tagline': 'Connecting Addition and Subtraction Fact Families', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-cyan-700', 'badge': 'Inverse Relationship', 'audio_text': 'Addition and subtraction are inverse operations: they are two sides of the very same mathematical coin! Addition puts groups together to find the whole, while subtraction takes a part away from the whole to find the other part. Whenever you know an addition fact, you instantly get two related subtraction facts for free! For instance, consider the addition fact: 125 plus 75 equals 200. From this single fact, we immediately know that: 200 minus 75 equals 125, and 200 minus 125 equals 75! This relationship is an invaluable tool for every traveller and engineer. Whenever you solve a subtraction problem, you can verify your answer with 100% certainty by adding your difference back to the smaller number. If the sum equals the original starting number, your calculation is proven correct! Inverse thinking transforms arithmetic into self-checking magic.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Fact Family Triangle', 'content': 'Three related numbers form a fact family: Part 1 + Part 2 = Whole; Whole - Part 1 = Part 2; Whole - Part 2 = Part 1.', 'icon': '🔺'}, {'title': 'Verification by Addition', 'content': 'To check subtraction: Difference + Subtrahend = Minuend. If 450 - 180 = 270, then check: 270 + 180 = 450!', 'icon': '✅'}, {'title': 'Missing Addend Problems', 'content': 'If a vehicle needs 50 litres and currently has 28 litres: 28 + ? = 50. We solve it using subtraction: 50 - 28 = 22 litres.', 'icon': '❓'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which subtraction sentence is directly derived from the addition fact 140 + 85 = 225?', 'options': ['225 - 85 = 140', '225 + 85 = 310', '140 - 85 = 55', '225 - 140 = 225'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Whole (225) minus Part (85) equals Part (140).'}, {'question': 'You calculate 500 - 320 = 180. Which addition confirms your answer is correct?', 'options': ['180 + 320 = 500', '500 + 320 = 820', '180 + 500 = 680', '320 - 180 = 140'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Difference (180) + Subtrahend (320) = Minuend (500).'}]}, {'id': 5, 'heading': 'Fuel Problems', 'tagline': 'Practical Word Problems, Regrouping, and Verification', 'color': 'from-purple-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Applied Arithmetic', 'audio_text': 'Let us apply our skills to real-world travel word problems from our textbook! Consider this practical scenario: A school tour minibus starts the morning with 38 litres of diesel in its fuel tank. At a highway fuel pump, the driver fills the tank completely until it reaches its full capacity of 65 litres. How many litres of diesel were added at the fuel pump? This is a classic missing-part problem: 38 litres plus the added fuel equals 65 litres. To find the added fuel, we subtract: 65 minus 38! In the Ones column, we have 5 minus 8. Since 5 is smaller than 8, we regroup: borrow 1 Ten from the 6 Tens, leaving 5 Tens. The borrowed 1 Ten equals 10 Ones, making 10 plus 5 equal to 15 Ones! Now subtract: 15 minus 8 equals 7 Ones. In the Tens column, 5 minus 3 equals 2 Tens. The answer is 27 litres! Let us check: 38 plus 27 equals 65 litres. Perfectly verified!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Analyzing Word Problems', 'content': 'Step 1: Identify given quantities (Initial = 38 L, Final = 65 L). Step 2: Determine operation (Final - Initial = Added). Step 3: Calculate with regrouping.', 'icon': '📝'}, {'title': 'Subtraction Regrouping (Borrowing)', 'content': 'When the top digit is smaller than the bottom digit, borrow 1 from the next higher place value: 1 Ten becomes 10 Ones.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Always Verify', 'content': 'Check your answer: Initial Fuel (38) + Added Fuel (27) = 65. Verification gives 100% confidence.', 'icon': '🔍'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': "A truck's fuel tank has a capacity of 120 litres. It currently contains 74 litres. How much fuel is needed to fill it completely?", 'options': ['46 litres', '56 litres', '36 litres', '54 litres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 120 - 74 = 46 litres. Check: 74 + 46 = 120.'}, {'question': 'A traveller started with ₹ 850 and spent ₹ 365 on vehicle fuel. How much money remains?', 'options': ['₹ 485', '₹ 495', '₹ 515', '₹ 385'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 850 - 365 = 485. Check: 485 + 365 = 850.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'heading': 'Consecutive Numbers', 'tagline': 'Discovering Sums, Symmetries, and Middle-Number Multipliers', 'color': 'from-rose-600 to-pink-700', 'badge': 'Number Pattern Investigation', 'audio_text': 'Now let us examine a captivating pattern from our textbook: sums of consecutive numbers! Consecutive numbers are whole numbers that follow one after another in order without skipping, such as 4, 5, and 6, or 21, 22, and 23. Look what happens when we add 3 consecutive numbers: Take 1, 2, and 3: their sum is 1 plus 2 plus 3, which equals 6. Notice that 6 is exactly 3 times the middle number, 2! Try 4, 5, and 6: their sum is 4 plus 5 plus 6, which equals 15. 15 is exactly 3 times the middle number, 5! Why does this always happen? Because 4 is 1 less than 5, and 6 is 1 more than 5. The minus 1 and plus 1 cancel out perfectly! Therefore, the sum of any 3 consecutive numbers is always 3 times the middle number. What about consecutive groups of 4 numbers, such as 1, 2, 3, and 4? Their sum is 10, an even number. For any 4 consecutive numbers, the sum is always an even number, and consecutive 4-number sums increase by 4 each step! These mathematical symmetries make mental calculation effortless.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '3 Consecutive Numbers Rule', 'content': 'For numbers (n-1), n, (n+1): Sum = 3 × Middle Number (n). Example: 10 + 11 + 12 = 3 × 11 = 33.', 'icon': '🔢'}, {'title': '4 Consecutive Numbers Rule', 'content': 'Sum of 4 consecutive numbers is always an even number! Example: 1+2+3+4 = 10; 2+3+4+5 = 14; 3+4+5+6 = 18. Each step increases by 4.', 'icon': '➕'}, {'title': 'Symmetry & Balance', 'content': 'Numbers equidistant from the centre pair up to equal sums. In 1, 2, 3, 4, 5: 1+5 = 6, 2+4 = 6, middle is 3. Total = 5 × 3 = 15.', 'icon': '✨'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the sum of 3 consecutive numbers whose middle number is 25?', 'options': ['75 (3 × 25)', '70', '80', '50'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Sum = 3 × Middle Number = 3 × 25 = 75 (24 + 25 + 26 = 75).'}, {'question': 'The sum of 3 consecutive numbers is 90. What is the middle number?', 'options': ['30 (90 / 3)', '29', '31', '28'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Middle number = 90 / 3 = 30. The numbers are 29, 30, and 31.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'heading': 'Longest Route', 'tagline': 'Adding 4-Digit and 5-Digit Highway Corridor Distances', 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-yellow-600', 'badge': 'Large Number Addition', 'audio_text': 'India is a vast and magnificent land with some of the longest highway networks in the world! Our textbook highlights the famous North-South Corridor, stretching all the way from Srinagar in Jammu and Kashmir in the north down to Kanyakumari at the southern tip of Tamil Nadu. This historic route spans approximately 4,000 kilometres! When adding multi-leg travel journeys with 4-digit and 5-digit numbers, we expand our place value chart to include Thousands and Ten Thousands. Suppose a fleet vehicle travels 1,284 kilometres in Leg 1, 2,476 kilometres in Leg 2, and 1,850 kilometres in Leg 3. To find the total distance, align the digits in the Th, H, T, and O columns. Add Ones: 4 + 6 + 0 = 10 (write 0, carry 1 Ten). Add Tens: 8 + 7 + 5 + 1 = 21 (write 1, carry 2 Hundreds). Add Hundreds: 2 + 4 + 8 + 2 = 16 (write 6, carry 1 Thousand). Add Thousands: 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 5 Thousands. The grand total is 5,610 kilometres! Regrouping across thousands follows the exact same systematic rule.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'North-South Corridor', 'content': 'Connecting Srinagar (J&K) to Kanyakumari (TN), National Highway 44 (NH 44) spans 4,112 km, forming the backbone of Indian road transport.', 'icon': '🛣️'}, {'title': 'Multi-Digit Column Carrying', 'content': 'Carry-overs occur when sum >= 10: 10 Tens = 1 Hundred; 10 Hundreds = 1 Thousand; 10 Thousands = 1 Ten Thousand.', 'icon': '📈'}, {'title': 'Route Leg Summation', 'content': 'Total Expedition Distance = Leg 1 + Leg 2 + Leg 3. Always double-check by adding columns in reverse order (bottom to top).', 'icon': '🗺️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 3,425 km + 2,780 km?', 'options': ['6,205 km', '6,105 km', '5,205 km', '6,215 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Ones: 5+0=5; Tens: 2+8=10 (carry 1); Hundreds: 4+7+1=12 (carry 1); Thousands: 3+2+1=6. Result = 6,205 km.'}, {'question': 'A truck travels 4,500 km in Week 1 and 3,650 km in Week 2. What is the total distance?', 'options': ['8,150 km', '7,150 km', '8,250 km', '8,050 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 4,500 + 3,650 = 8,150 km.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'heading': 'Route Distances', 'tagline': 'Subtracting Large Distances Across Sea Ports and Highways', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Large Number Subtraction', 'audio_text': "Just as we add route legs to calculate total journeys, we subtract large distances to compare different travel options! Consider India's major maritime shipping ports: Mumbai on the western coast, Cochin in the south, and Chennai on the eastern coast. Suppose a cargo ship route from Port A to Port B is 3,240 nautical miles, while a modern direct coastal route is 2,165 nautical miles. How much distance does the direct coastal route save? We calculate: 3,240 minus 2,165! Look at the Ones column: 0 minus 5 is not possible, so we borrow 1 Ten from 4 Tens, leaving 3 Tens. The 0 becomes 10 Ones. 10 minus 5 equals 5 Ones. In Tens: 3 minus 6 is not possible, so borrow 1 Hundred from 2 Hundreds, leaving 1 Hundred. 13 minus 6 equals 7 Tens. In Hundreds: 1 minus 1 equals 0. In Thousands: 3 minus 2 equals 1 Thousand. The direct route saves 1,075 nautical miles! Borrowing across multiple place value columns follows the exact same logical chain.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Coastal Sea Routes', 'content': 'India has a 7,516 km coastline. Comparing nautical distances between Mumbai, Cochin, Chennai, and Kolkata reveals huge fuel savings.', 'icon': '⚓'}, {'title': 'Multi-Column Borrowing', 'content': 'When borrowing cascades across zeros (e.g. 5,000 - 2,345), borrow from the nearest non-zero column to the left, turning intermediate zeros into 9s.', 'icon': '⛓️'}, {'title': 'Distance Comparison', 'content': 'Difference = Longer Route - Shorter Route. The difference represents saved kilometres, reduced travel hours, and conserved fuel.', 'icon': '📏'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 5,000 km - 3,240 km?', 'options': ['1,760 km', '1,860 km', '2,760 km', '1,740 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 5,000 - 3,240 = 1,760 km. Check: 1,760 + 3,240 = 5,000 km.'}, {'question': 'Route 1 is 8,450 km and Route 2 is 6,800 km. How much longer is Route 1?', 'options': ['1,650 km', '1,550 km', '2,650 km', '1,600 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 8,450 - 6,800 = 1,650 km.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'heading': 'Mental Math', 'tagline': 'Quick Shortcuts to Powers of Ten and Mental Adjustments', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Mental Calculation Strategies', 'audio_text': 'Speed in calculation is the hallmark of a confident mathematician! Our textbook introduces powerful mental math shortcuts to calculate sums and differences without pencil and paper. The first strategy is rounding to the nearest power of ten. Suppose you need to add 99 to 345. Rather than doing column addition, think: 99 is just 1 less than 100! First add 100: 345 plus 100 is 445. Then subtract the extra 1: 445 minus 1 is 444! You get the exact answer in a single breath. What if you need to subtract 9 from 83? 9 is 1 less than 10. Subtract 10 to get 73, then add back 1 to get 74! The same shortcut applies to 999: add 1,000 and subtract 1. Another mental technique is making tens and hundreds: to add 68 and 37, take 2 from 37 and give it to 68 to make a friendly 70! Now add 70 and 35 to get 105 instantly. Mental math transforms numbers into flexible building blocks.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Adding 9, 99, 999 (+100 - 1)', 'content': 'To add 99: Add 100, then subtract 1. Example: 456 + 99 = (456 + 100) - 1 = 556 - 1 = 555.', 'icon': '⚡'}, {'title': 'Subtracting 9, 99 (-100 + 1)', 'content': 'To subtract 99: Subtract 100, then add 1. Example: 350 - 99 = (350 - 100) + 1 = 250 + 1 = 251.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Friendly Number Transfer', 'content': 'To add 198 + 45: Take 2 from 45 and add to 198 to create 200! Then 200 + 43 = 243 in seconds.', 'icon': '🤝'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Using the mental shortcut, what is 267 + 99?', 'options': ['366 (267 + 100 - 1)', '367', '365', '356'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 267 + 100 = 367, minus 1 = 366.'}, {'question': 'What is 142 - 9 using mental adjustment?', 'options': ['133 (142 - 10 + 1)', '131', '135', '132'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 142 - 10 = 132, plus 1 = 133.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'heading': 'Palindrome Numbers', 'tagline': 'Exploring Symmetrical Numbers That Read the Same Forwards and Backwards', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Number Pattern Wonder', 'audio_text': 'Look at the word NOON or the name NITIN: whether you read them from left to right or right to left, they read exactly the same! In mathematics, numbers with this magical forward-backward symmetry are called palindrome numbers! A palindrome number reads identically in both directions. Single-digit numbers like 7 are trivially palindromes. In two-digit numbers, the palindromes are numbers with identical digits: 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, and 99! In three-digit numbers, the hundreds digit and the ones digit must match, such as 121, 363, 505, 787, and 999. In four-digit numbers, the outer two digits match and the inner two digits match, such as 2,442 or 8,558! Did you know that many ordinary numbers can be turned into palindromes through a reverse-and-add routine? Take 43: reverse its digits to get 34. Now add: 43 plus 34 equals 77, an instant palindrome! Palindromic patterns reveal the poetic symmetry hidden within arithmetic.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Definition of Palindrome', 'content': 'A number that remains unchanged when its digits are reversed. Examples: 55, 121, 383, 2,332, 54,445.', 'icon': '🪞'}, {'title': '3-Digit Palindrome Structure', 'content': 'A 3-digit palindrome has the form ABA, where the first and last digits (Hundreds and Ones) are identical (A != 0).', 'icon': '🔢'}, {'title': 'The Reverse-and-Add Trick', 'content': 'Start with a number: e.g. 52. Reverse digits: 25. Add them: 52 + 25 = 77! 77 is a palindrome.', 'icon': '🪄'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which of the following numbers is a palindrome?', 'options': ['484', '485', '348', '488'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 484 reversed is 484, reading identically forward and backward.'}, {'question': 'How many 2-digit palindrome numbers exist in total (from 10 to 99)?', 'options': ['9 (11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99)', '10', '8', '11'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! There are exactly 9 two-digit palindromes: 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, and 99.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'heading': 'Grid Puzzles', 'tagline': 'Arranging Numbers 1 Through 9 in Monotonic 3×3 Matrices', 'color': 'from-violet-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Mathematical Logic Puzzle', 'audio_text': 'Here is an intriguing challenge from our textbook: arranging the numbers 1 through 9 inside a 3 by 3 grid! The rule requires that every single row from left to right must follow an increasing order, and every single column from top to bottom must also follow an increasing order! No number from 1 to 9 may be repeated. Where must the smallest number, 1, be placed? Because 1 is smaller than every other number, it must be smaller than everything to its right and everything below it. Therefore, 1 must always occupy the top-left corner! Similarly, where must the largest number, 9, reside? Because 9 is greater than all other numbers, it must be at the end of its row and the bottom of its column: 9 must occupy the bottom-right corner! The remaining numbers 2 through 8 can be arranged in several valid configurations as long as both horizontal and vertical increasing conditions are satisfied. Grid puzzles train your brain to think with spatial constraints and monotonic ordering.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Monotonic Grid Rule', 'content': 'For every cell (r, c): Cell(r, c) < Cell(r, c+1) (increasing across rows) and Cell(r, c) < Cell(r+1, c) (increasing down columns).', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Corner Invariants', 'content': 'Top-Left cell MUST be 1 (global minimum). Bottom-Right cell MUST be 9 (global maximum).', 'icon': '📌'}, {'title': 'Valid Solutions', 'content': 'Standard reading grid: Row 1: [1, 2, 3]; Row 2: [4, 5, 6]; Row 3: [7, 8, 9]. Another valid setup: Row 1: [1, 2, 4]; Row 2: [3, 5, 7]; Row 3: [6, 8, 9].', 'icon': '🧩'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'In a 3×3 grid of numbers 1 to 9 with strictly increasing rows and columns, what number must be in the top-left cell?', 'options': ['1', '5', '9', '3'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! As the smallest number (global minimum), 1 must be placed at the top-left.'}, {'question': 'Which number must be placed in the bottom-right cell of the increasing 3×3 grid?', 'options': ['9', '8', '7', '5'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 9 is the largest number and must terminate both the last row and last column.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'heading': 'Even and Odd', 'tagline': 'Paired Dot Arrangements and the Laws of Sum Parity', 'color': 'from-cyan-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Number Parity Principles', 'audio_text': 'What makes a number even or odd? The secret lies in pairing! An even number can be arranged into complete pairs of two with nothing left over. Any number ending in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 is an even number. An odd number, when arranged in pairs, always leaves exactly one solitary dot without a partner! Any number ending in 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9 is an odd number. Now observe what happens when we add pairs of numbers: Even plus Even equals Even! Two complete sets of pairs join together to make a larger set of complete pairs. For example, 4 plus 6 equals 10! Odd plus Odd also equals Even! Why? Because the two lonely leftover dots pair up with each other to form a new complete pair! For example, 3 plus 5 equals 8! Even plus Odd always equals Odd, because the single leftover dot has nobody to pair with. For example, 4 plus 5 equals 9! These parity laws allow us to verify sums and predict outcomes instantaneously.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Pairing Test', 'content': 'Even = Divisible by 2, zero remainder (complete pairs). Odd = Leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 2 (one leftover item).', 'icon': '👥'}, {'title': 'The 3 Addition Parity Laws', 'content': '1. Even + Even = Even (e.g. 6 + 8 = 14); 2. Odd + Odd = Even (e.g. 5 + 7 = 12); 3. Even + Odd = Odd (e.g. 4 + 7 = 11).', 'icon': '⚖️'}, {'title': 'Consecutive Parity', 'content': 'Consecutive numbers always alternate: Even, Odd, Even, Odd... Therefore, adding any 2 consecutive numbers always gives an ODD sum (Even + Odd = Odd)!', 'icon': '🔄'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the parity of the sum when you add two odd numbers (e.g., 7 + 9)?', 'options': ['Always Even', 'Always Odd', 'Sometimes Even, Sometimes Odd', 'Zero'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! The two leftover dots from the odd numbers pair up, making the total sum strictly Even (7 + 9 = 16).'}, {'question': 'You add an even number and an odd number (e.g., 8 + 5). What is the resulting sum?', 'options': ['Always Odd', 'Always Even', 'A negative number', 'A multiple of 10'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Even + Odd is always Odd (8 + 5 = 13).'}]}, {'id': 13, 'heading': 'Logic Puzzles', 'tagline': 'Mastering Coin Combinations and Torch Switch Parity', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-green-700', 'badge': 'Deductive Reasoning', 'audio_text': 'Our textbook presents delightful thinking puzzles involving coin combinations and torch switches! In the coin puzzle: Suppose you have a total of ₹ 25 made up of 2-rupee and 5-rupee coins. How many coins of each type could you have? Notice that ₹ 25 is an odd number. Any number of 2-rupee coins will always give an even sum. Therefore, the 5-rupee coins must contribute an odd sum, meaning there must be an odd number of 5-rupee coins! If we take one 5-rupee coin, the remaining ₹ 20 requires ten 2-rupee coins: 10 times 2 plus 5 equals 25! If we take three 5-rupee coins, that gives ₹ 15, and the remaining ₹ 10 requires five 2-rupee coins! Now look at the torch switch puzzle: A flashlight switch toggles between ON and OFF. Starting from OFF, press 1 turns it ON. Press 2 turns it OFF. Every odd press leaves the torch ON; every even press leaves the torch OFF! If you press the switch 47 times, 47 is an odd number, so the torch will be ON! Mathematical parity turns tricky puzzles into crystal-clear logic.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Coin Combination Analysis', 'content': 'To get ₹ 25 from ₹ 2 and ₹ 5 coins: 2a + 5b = 25. Since 2a is even and 25 is odd, 5b must be odd. Hence b must be odd (b = 1, 3, or 5).', 'icon': '🪙'}, {'title': 'Torch Switch Parity', 'content': 'Starting state: OFF. Press 1 (Odd) = ON; Press 2 (Even) = OFF; Press 3 (Odd) = ON... Press N: Odd N = ON, Even N = OFF.', 'icon': '🔦'}, {'title': 'Binary State Logic', 'content': 'Two-state systems (ON/OFF, Open/Closed, Heads/Tails) obey modulo-2 arithmetic: State = Count % 2.', 'icon': '💡'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A torch is initially turned OFF. If the button is pressed 35 times, what state is the torch in?', 'options': ['ON (35 is an odd number)', 'OFF (35 is an even number)', 'Flickering half-way', 'Completely broken'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Starting from OFF, every odd number of presses leaves the torch ON.'}, {'question': 'Can you make exactly ₹ 25 using only 2-rupee coins?', 'options': ['No, because any number of 2-rupee coins produces an even sum, and 25 is odd', 'Yes, using 12 coins', 'Yes, using 13 coins', 'Yes, using 12.5 coins'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 2 × any whole number is always even, so ₹ 25 (odd) cannot be made with ₹ 2 coins alone.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'heading': 'Mountain Peaks', 'tagline': "Statistical Analysis of Indian Mountaineer Priyanka Mohite's Expeditions", 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-orange-700', 'badge': 'Data Analysis & Altitudes', 'audio_text': 'Let us celebrate the extraordinary achievements of Indian mountaineer Priyanka Mohite, featured in our textbook! Priyanka Mohite is the first Indian woman to climb five peaks above 8,000 metres! Look at the majestic Himalayan peaks she scaled: Mount Everest stands at 8,849 metres above sea level. Kanchenjunga, the third highest peak on Earth located on the India-Nepal border, reaches 8,586 metres. Makalu rises to 8,485 metres, Lhotse to 8,516 metres, and Annapurna to 8,091 metres. Comparing these colossal heights gives us rich real-world subtraction practice! How much taller is Mount Everest than Mount Annapurna? We subtract: 8,849 minus 8,091! 9 minus 1 is 8 Ones. 4 minus 9 in the Tens requires borrowing from 8 Hundreds, leaving 7 Hundreds: 14 minus 9 is 5 Tens. 7 minus 0 is 7 Hundreds. 8 minus 8 is 0 Thousands. Everest is 758 metres taller than Annapurna! Mathematics allows us to measure and appreciate the highest points on our planet.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': "Priyanka Mohite's 8,000m Record", 'content': 'Recipient of the Tenzing Norgay National Adventure Award, she summited Everest (8,849 m), Kanchenjunga (8,586 m), Lhotse (8,516 m), Makalu (8,485 m), and Annapurna (8,091 m).', 'icon': '🏔️'}, {'title': 'Elevation Comparison', 'content': 'Height Difference = Peak A - Peak B. Example: Everest (8,849 m) - Kanchenjunga (8,586 m) = 263 metres.', 'icon': '📊'}, {'title': 'Ascent Math', 'content': "Climbers establish high-altitude camps (Base Camp, Camp 1, Camp 2, Camp 3). Each day's elevation gain is calculated using subtraction.", 'icon': '🧗\u200d♀️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Mount Everest is 8,849 metres and Kanchenjunga is 8,586 metres. What is the difference in height?', 'options': ['263 metres', '363 metres', '243 metres', '163 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 8,849 - 8,586 = 263 metres. Check: 8,586 + 263 = 8,849 m.'}, {'question': 'A mountaineer climbs from Base Camp at 5,364 m to Camp 1 at 6,065 m. How many vertical metres did they gain?', 'options': ['701 metres', '601 metres', '801 metres', '711 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 6,065 - 5,364 = 701 vertical metres.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'heading': 'Math Mela', 'tagline': 'District Competition Data: Certificates, Cricket Runs, and Factory Records', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Comprehensive Review', 'audio_text': "We conclude our chapter's core journey with the grand Math Metric Mela! A district-level math festival brings together students, teachers, and schools for fun competitions. Let us examine the festival's event data: The organizing committee printed 2,500 certificates of participation. On the day of the Mela, 1,875 students attended and received certificates. How many surplus certificates were left over? We subtract: 2,500 minus 1,875! Borrowing across zeros gives: 2,500 minus 1,875 equals 625 certificates remaining. In the cricket pavilion, Team A scored 348 runs and Team B scored 295 runs. The total runs scored in the match were 348 plus 295, which equals 643 runs! And in savings arithmetic: if a student saves ₹ 45 in Week 1, ₹ 60 in Week 2, and ₹ 75 in Week 3, their total savings equal ₹ 180. Every real-life event, from sports to festivals, runs on solid addition and subtraction.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Certificate Inventory Math', 'content': 'Printed (2,500) - Distributed (1,875) = Surplus Remaining (625 certificates). Always verify: 1,875 + 625 = 2,500.', 'icon': '📜'}, {'title': 'Cricket Match Aggregate', 'content': 'Match Total Runs = Team A Runs + Team B Runs. Example: 348 + 295 = 643 runs.', 'icon': '🏏'}, {'title': 'Cumulative Weekly Savings', 'content': 'Total Savings = Week 1 + Week 2 + Week 3... Tracking personal savings reinforces consistent budgeting and financial literacy.', 'icon': '💰'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A school ordered 1,500 medals for sports day. 1,180 medals were awarded to winners. How many medals remained?', 'options': ['320 medals', '420 medals', '220 medals', '380 medals'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 1,500 - 1,180 = 320 medals.'}, {'question': 'A cricketer scored 136 runs in the first innings and 89 runs in the second innings. What was their total score?', 'options': ['225 runs', '215 runs', '235 runs', '220 runs'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 136 + 89 = 225 total runs.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'heading': 'Travel Lab', 'tagline': 'Integrated Multi-Leg Expedition and Fuel Expense Calculator', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Expedition Synthesis Sandbox', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to the ultimate Travel Arithmetic Laboratory! Here, all the concepts we have mastered come together into an interactive expedition planner. You can choose a travel route across India, select your transport vehicle—from an eco-friendly bus to an electric train or family car—and dynamically calculate total journey distance, fuel consumed in litres, and total travel cost. You can also test our palindrome detector by typing any odometer reading to see if it forms a symmetrical palindrome number! Notice how increasing the number of passengers traveling together in a bus drastically lowers the fuel and carbon footprint per person. Experiment with multi-leg journeys, verify route subtractions, and test mental math shortcuts. You now hold the power of mathematical travel planning in your hands!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Multi-Leg Journey Synthesis', 'content': 'Total Distance = Leg 1 + Leg 2 + Leg 3. Total Fuel = Total Distance / Vehicle Mileage. Total Cost = Fuel Litres × Fuel Price.', 'icon': '🗺️'}, {'title': 'Per-Capita Fuel Footprint', 'content': 'Fuel per Passenger = Total Fuel / Passengers. Taking a bus with 40 people cuts individual fuel consumption by up to 80% compared to a solo car trip.', 'icon': '👥'}, {'title': 'Live Palindrome Checker', 'content': 'Any odometer distance (e.g. 12,321 km) can be verified instantly for front-to-back palindromic symmetry.', 'icon': '🪞'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A vehicle travels a total distance of 450 km. If its mileage is 15 km per litre, how many litres of fuel are needed?', 'options': ['30 litres (450 / 15)', '25 litres', '35 litres', '45 litres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Fuel needed = 450 km / 15 km/L = 30 litres.'}, {'question': 'If diesel costs ₹ 90 per litre, what is the cost of 30 litres of diesel?', 'options': ['₹ 2,700', '₹ 2,400', '₹ 3,000', '₹ 2,600'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 30 litres × ₹ 90 = ₹ 2,700.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'heading': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Equal Sums, Fuel Mileage, Place Value, and Inverse Facts', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 1', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 1! Test your foundational mastery of balancing equal sums, vehicle fuel mileage, column addition with place value regrouping, and inverse fact families. Examine each question carefully, choose the correct answer, and review the detailed step-by-step solution modal for complete mathematical clarity.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'Group A sums to 54 and Group B sums to 42. By what difference must two swapped numbers differ to balance both groups?', 'options': ['6 (Difference of 12 divided by 2)', '12', '3', '24'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Total difference = 54 - 42 = 12. To balance the groups in a single swap, the swapped numbers must differ by 12 / 2 = 6. Target sum for each group is (54 + 42) / 2 = 48.'}, {'question': 'A motorcycle travels 48 kilometres on 1 litre of petrol. How far can it travel on 4 litres?', 'options': ['192 km', '182 km', '202 km', '148 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Distance = 48 km/L × 4 L = 192 km.'}, {'question': 'What is the sum of 458 and 276?', 'options': ['734', '724', '744', '634'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Ones: 8 + 6 = 14 (write 4, carry 1); Tens: 5 + 7 + 1 = 13 (write 3, carry 1); Hundreds: 4 + 2 + 1 = 7. Total = 734.'}, {'question': 'Which subtraction sentence is derived from the addition fact 260 + 175 = 435?', 'options': ['435 - 175 = 260', '435 + 175 = 610', '260 - 175 = 85', '435 - 260 = 435'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'From Part + Part = Whole (260 + 175 = 435), the inverse subtraction is Whole - Part = Part (435 - 175 = 260).'}, {'question': 'A car consumes 20 litres of petrol for a 300 km trip. What is its fuel mileage in km per litre?', 'options': ['15 km/L (300 / 20)', '20 km/L', '12 km/L', '25 km/L'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Mileage = Distance / Fuel = 300 km / 20 L = 15 km/L.'}, {'question': 'When adding Tens column, the sum is 15. What is written in the Tens place, and what is carried over?', 'options': ['Write 5 in Tens, carry 1 to Hundreds', 'Write 1 in Tens, carry 5 to Hundreds', 'Write 15 in Tens', 'Carry 15 to Hundreds'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '15 Tens = 1 Hundred and 5 Tens. We write 5 in the Tens column and carry over 1 to Hundreds.'}, {'question': 'If 720 - 450 = 270, what addition confirms this calculation?', 'options': ['270 + 450 = 720', '720 + 450 = 1,170', '270 + 720 = 990', '450 - 270 = 180'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Checking subtraction: Difference + Subtrahend = Minuend. 270 + 450 = 720.'}, {'question': 'Group A has cards [10, 20, 30] summing to 60. Group B has cards [15, 25, 40] summing to 80. What is the target balanced sum for each group?', 'options': ['70 (Total 140 / 2)', '75', '65', '80'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Total combined sum = 60 + 80 = 140. Balanced target = 140 / 2 = 70.'}, {'question': 'Why is travelling by passenger train considered fuel-efficient?', 'options': ['It carries hundreds of travellers simultaneously, minimizing fuel used per person', 'It does not travel any distance', 'It never stops at stations', 'It only carries cargo'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'High passenger capacity spreads the total energy consumption across hundreds of travellers, drastically lowering per-capita emissions.'}, {'question': 'Find the missing number: ? + 145 = 300.', 'options': ['155', '165', '145', '150'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Missing addend = 300 - 145 = 155.'}]}, {'id': 18, 'heading': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Fuel Problems, Consecutive Numbers, and Large Route Calculations', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 2', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 2! Test your understanding of fuel word problems, consecutive number sum properties, and large multi-digit corridor distances. Calculate each step methodically and inspect the solution modal whenever you need guidance.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'A bus fuel tank holds 80 litres. It currently contains 47 litres. How many litres are required to fill it completely?', 'options': ['33 litres', '43 litres', '23 litres', '37 litres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Fuel required = 80 - 47 = 33 litres. Check: 47 + 33 = 80 L.'}, {'question': 'What is the sum of 3 consecutive numbers whose middle number is 18?', 'options': ['54 (3 × 18)', '52', '56', '48'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'For 3 consecutive numbers, Sum = 3 × Middle Number = 3 × 18 = 54 (17 + 18 + 19 = 54).'}, {'question': 'What is 4,325 km + 3,890 km?', 'options': ['8,215 km', '8,115 km', '7,215 km', '8,225 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Ones: 5+0=5; Tens: 2+9=11 (carry 1); Hundreds: 3+8+1=12 (carry 1); Thousands: 4+3+1=8. Result = 8,215 km.'}, {'question': 'A cargo route is 6,400 km and a direct express highway is 4,850 km. How much distance does the highway save?', 'options': ['1,550 km', '1,650 km', '2,550 km', '1,450 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Distance saved = 6,400 - 4,850 = 1,550 km.'}, {'question': 'The sum of 3 consecutive numbers is 105. What are the three numbers?', 'options': ['34, 35, 36 (Middle = 105 / 3 = 35)', '33, 34, 35', '35, 36, 37', '32, 35, 38'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Middle number = 105 / 3 = 35. The consecutive numbers are 34, 35, and 36.'}, {'question': 'What is the parity of the sum of any 4 consecutive numbers (e.g. 1+2+3+4 = 10)?', 'options': ['Always Even', 'Always Odd', 'Zero', 'Prime'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Four consecutive numbers contain two odd and two even numbers: Odd + Odd = Even, and Even + Even = Even, so the total sum is strictly Even.'}, {'question': 'Calculate: 12,450 + 8,765.', 'options': ['21,215', '20,215', '21,115', '21,315'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Ones: 0+5=5; Tens: 5+6=11 (carry 1); Hundreds: 4+7+1=12 (carry 1); Thousands: 2+8+1=11 (carry 1); Ten Thousands: 1+1=2. Total = 21,215.'}, {'question': 'A tourist bus covers 340 km on Day 1, 415 km on Day 2, and 285 km on Day 3. What is the total distance covered?', 'options': ['1,040 km', '1,030 km', '940 km', '1,140 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '340 + 415 + 285 = 340 + 700 = 1,040 km.'}, {'question': 'Calculate: 8,000 - 4,325.', 'options': ['3,675', '3,775', '4,675', '3,685'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Borrowing across zeros: 8,000 - 4,325 = 3,675. Check: 3,675 + 4,325 = 8,000.'}, {'question': 'What is the sum of 4 consecutive numbers: 11, 12, 13, and 14?', 'options': ['50', '48', '52', '46'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '11 + 12 + 13 + 14 = 23 + 27 = 50 (an even number!).'}]}, {'id': 19, 'heading': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Mental Math Shortcuts, Palindromes, and 3×3 Grid Puzzles', 'color': 'from-cyan-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 3', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 3! Dive into rapid mental calculation shortcuts, symmetrical palindrome numbers, and spatial 3×3 matrix puzzles. Solve each problem with speed and precision, and check your reasoning with the solution modal.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is the mental shortcut to add 99 to any number?', 'options': ['Add 100, then subtract 1', 'Add 90, then add 9', 'Subtract 100, then add 1', 'Multiply by 99'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Since 99 = 100 - 1, adding 99 is identical to adding 100 and subtracting 1.'}, {'question': 'Calculate mentally: 538 + 99.', 'options': ['637 (538 + 100 - 1)', '638', '636', '628'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '538 + 100 = 638, and 638 - 1 = 637.'}, {'question': 'Calculate mentally: 275 - 99.', 'options': ['176 (275 - 100 + 1)', '175', '174', '186'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '275 - 100 = 175, and 175 + 1 = 176.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following is a 4-digit palindrome number?', 'options': ['3,553', '3,535', '5,335', '3,355'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '3,553 reversed is 3,553. The first and last digits match (3), and the middle two digits match (5).'}, {'question': 'How many 3-digit palindrome numbers start and end with the digit 2 (form 2_2)?', 'options': ['10 (202, 212, 222, 232, 242, 252, 262, 272, 282, 292)', '9', '8', '5'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'The middle tens digit can be any digit from 0 to 9: exactly 10 palindromes.'}, {'question': 'In a 3×3 grid of numbers 1 to 9 with increasing rows and columns, what number MUST be at top-left?', 'options': ['1', '5', '9', '3'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1 is the smallest number (global minimum) and must be placed at the top-left corner.'}, {'question': 'In the same increasing 3×3 grid, which number MUST occupy the bottom-right corner?', 'options': ['9', '8', '7', '6'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '9 is the largest number (global maximum) and must terminate the last row and column at bottom-right.'}, {'question': 'Start with 63. Reverse its digits (36) and add them together. What palindrome do you get?', 'options': ['99 (63 + 36)', '88', '101', '77'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '63 + 36 = 99, a two-digit palindrome number.'}, {'question': 'Calculate mentally: 470 + 999.', 'options': ['1,469 (470 + 1,000 - 1)', '1,470', '1,468', '1,459'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '470 + 1,000 = 1,470; 1,470 - 1 = 1,469.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following grid rows is NOT in strictly increasing order?', 'options': ['[4, 6, 5]', '[2, 5, 8]', '[1, 3, 7]', '[3, 4, 9]'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'In [4, 6, 5], 6 is followed by 5 (decreasing), which violates the increasing order rule.'}]}, {'id': 20, 'heading': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Parity Laws, Coin Puzzles, Switch Logic, and Mountain Elevations', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 4', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to the final challenge! Test your advanced mathematical mastery of parity addition laws, coin combinations, binary switch states, and mountaineering altitude data. Solve each puzzle, verify your score, and celebrate your mastery of We the Travellers - II!', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is Even Number + Even Number always equal to?', 'options': ['Even Number', 'Odd Number', 'Zero', 'Prime Number'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Even + Even is always Even (e.g. 4 + 6 = 10, complete pairs remain complete pairs).'}, {'question': 'What is Odd Number + Odd Number always equal to?', 'options': ['Even Number', 'Odd Number', 'Fraction', 'Negative Number'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Odd + Odd is always Even (e.g. 5 + 7 = 12, the two leftover dots combine into a pair).'}, {'question': 'What is Even Number + Odd Number always equal to?', 'options': ['Odd Number', 'Even Number', 'Multiple of 4', 'Zero'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Even + Odd is always Odd (e.g. 6 + 5 = 11, one dot remains unpaired).'}, {'question': 'You have ₹ 25 made of 2-rupee and 5-rupee coins. Which combination is mathematically possible?', 'options': ['Ten 2-rupee coins and one 5-rupee coin (10×2 + 1×5 = 25)', 'Twelve 2-rupee coins and one 5-rupee coin', 'Eight 2-rupee coins and two 5-rupee coins', 'Five 2-rupee coins and three 5-rupee coins'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '10 × ₹ 2 = ₹ 20, plus 1 × ₹ 5 = ₹ 25. Another valid combination is 5 × ₹ 2 + 3 × ₹ 5 = 25.'}, {'question': 'A torch is initially OFF. You press the switch 50 times. What is its final state?', 'options': ['OFF (50 is an even number)', 'ON', 'Blinking', 'Half ON'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Starting from OFF, every even number of presses returns the switch to OFF.'}, {'question': 'Mount Everest is 8,849 metres and Makalu is 8,485 metres. What is the height difference?', 'options': ['364 metres', '354 metres', '464 metres', '264 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '8,849 - 8,485 = 364 metres.'}, {'question': 'Priyanka Mohite climbed Annapurna (8,091 m) and Lhotse (8,516 m). How much higher is Lhotse?', 'options': ['425 metres', '525 metres', '325 metres', '415 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '8,516 - 8,091 = 425 metres.'}, {'question': 'Can the sum of two consecutive numbers ever be an even number?', 'options': ['No, it is always Odd because one is Even and one is Odd', 'Yes, always Even', 'Yes, if both numbers are large', 'Yes, if both end in 5'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Two consecutive numbers always alternate (one even, one odd). Since Even + Odd = Odd, their sum is strictly Odd.'}, {'question': 'At the Math Mela, 3,000 visitor badges were printed and 2,460 were distributed. How many badges remained?', 'options': ['540 badges', '640 badges', '440 badges', '560 badges'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '3,000 - 2,460 = 540 badges remaining.'}, {'question': 'A student saves ₹ 50 in Week 1, ₹ 75 in Week 2, and ₹ 125 in Week 3. What are their total savings?', 'options': ['₹ 250', '₹ 240', '₹ 260', '₹ 225'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '50 + 75 + 125 = 50 + 200 = ₹ 250.'}]}]
